# Fixtures the benchmark does not compile, one name per line.
#
# This is not a list of things that are slow or uninteresting - it is the list of fixtures the
# compiler does not currently get through, and it exists because the benchmark has no expectations
# to fail against. The resolve driver stops at the first thing that does not hold together and
# reports it; this driver hands whatever came out to the next pass, and a program whose lower IR is
# already inconsistent takes the process down somewhere further along. Every name below is one the
# resolve suite is reporting a failure for today.
#
# So: it should shrink to nothing, and the way to regenerate it is
#
#     ../build-bench/test/YanaResolveTest 2>&1 \
#         | grep -oE "Fail \(resolve/[A-Za-z0-9._]+\.yana\)" \
#         | sed -e 's|Fail (resolve/||' -e 's|)||' | sort -u
#
# and it must be regenerated before a benchmark comparison rather than after: a corpus that changed
# between the two measurements is not a comparison. If the list is wrong in the direction of too
# many names the benchmark is only smaller than it could be; wrong in the other direction and the
# run stops partway with a segmentation fault and no output at all.
#
# It is currently empty, which is where it should stay. The fourteen names that were here were all
# one bug - the dangling subscript operand fixed in resolveSubscript - and the benchmark compiles
# the whole corpus again.
